<!-- ══ SECTION I — FOREWORD ══ -->
<section class="foreword">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="sec-head rev">
      <p class="overline">Section I — Prospectus &amp; Foreword</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">A Word from the Press</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="fore-grid">
      <div class="fore-text rev">
        <p class="dropcap">It is a well-known fact that no surname in the English-speaking world has been so generously distributed as Bill, and that none has been so lightly treated in verse. The present almanack remedies both omissions. Within these pages the reader will find twenty-five limericks, each concerning a different person of the name — a baker, a sea-captain, a chess player of some local repute, and one serious economist, whose forecasts were remarkably accurate and whose own bill, regrettably, he could never quite read.</p>
        <p>Verse IV will be of particular interest to readers of the continental correspondence, concerning the brief and regrettable dispute between a Bill of Billbrook and a certain Jürgen, who argued with a degree of debate that only the sea, at last, could resolve. The reader is invited to peruse the <a href="#contents-box">Contents</a> at his leisure, and to press the small <em>recite</em> button beside any verse, which will carry the eye down its five lines at a dignified pace. The Press apologises in advance for any resulting foot-tapping.</p>
        <p>Each verse is accompanied by a line of marginalia, contributed by the Press's bill collector, who saw to the printing and to the accounts with equal and, it must be said, unbreakable patience.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="aside-plate rev">
        <div class="plate-frame small">
          <div class="kb">
            <img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/bill-press-portrait-1887/440/560" alt="An engraved-style portrait of the Press's Bill">
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption><b>PLATE I.</b> — The Press's Bill, head. Engraved from a photograph taken on a Tuesday, and thereafter regretted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote rev"><sup>†</sup> No connection with the reader is implied, though several of the Bills above have asked to be told of.</p>
  </div>
</section>

